export default {
  "wow-forever-beginners-guide": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever Beginner's Guide (2026): How to Start Playing and What to Know\ndescription: How to start WoW Forever: pick a ruleset, class and faction, get through your first ten levels, earn gold, and follow the story of Azeroth as you go.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n*New to World of Warcraft: Forever? Here's everything you need to know before your first adventure, from choosing your class to leveling, earning gold, and discovering the world of Azeroth.*\n\nWorld of Warcraft: Forever launches on **November 4, 2026**, offering players a fresh journey through a reimagined version of the original Azeroth.\n\nWhether you've never played World of Warcraft or you're returning after years away, Forever is designed around something that made the original game memorable: the journey itself.\n\nYou'll explore unfamiliar roads, discover stories hidden throughout the world, fight dangerous creatures, complete quests, meet other adventurers, and gradually build a character of your own.\n\nBut where should you start?\n\nThis beginner's guide explains how WoW Forever works, which class to choose, how leveling works, and what new players should prioritize during their first hours.\n\n## What Is World of Warcraft: Forever?\n\nWorld of Warcraft: Forever is a new, permanent version of Blizzard's massively multiplayer online role-playing game (MMORPG).\n\nIt's inspired by the original World of Warcraft but introduces new content, updated classes, expanded character choices, new progression systems, and fresh adventures.\n\nUnlike modern World of Warcraft, where players often focus on reaching the latest endgame as quickly as possible, Forever puts greater emphasis on exploration, character progression, and the experience of living in Azeroth.\n\nThe game features:\n\n- A level cap of 60.\n- Nine playable classes with updated abilities and talents.\n- The new Skyborne race.\n- New zones, quests, dungeons, and future raids.\n- New systems including Legacy progression and Camping.\n- Updated environments and optional visual improvements.\n- A world designed to continue growing over time.\n\nForever also has its own Warcraft timeline, beginning after the events of Warcraft III: Forsaken Kingdom and before the original Molten Core raid.\n\nThat distinction matters. Not every story or event from modern WoW has happened in Forever's version of Azeroth.\n\n## When Does WoW Forever Launch, and How Do You Play?\n\nWoW Forever launches globally on **November 4, 2026, at 3:00 p.m. Pacific Standard Time**.\n\nAccess to the base game is included with an active World of Warcraft subscription or Game Time. You do not need to buy a special Forever edition simply to play at launch.\n\nBlizzard also offers optional paid upgrade editions with extra benefits. The new Skyborne race and its Zephras Isle starting experience require the Skyborne Heroic Pack or a higher edition.\n\nTo begin your adventure:\n\n1. Create a Battle.net account if you don't already have one.\n2. Download and install the Battle.net desktop app.\n3. Choose World of Warcraft and install the WoW Forever game version when available.\n4. Make sure your subscription or Game Time is active for launch.\n5. Create your character and choose how you want to experience Azeroth.\n\nOnce you're inside the game, your first quest will introduce you to your character's starting area and the basics of combat, movement, and exploration.\n\n## Choose the Right Ruleset\n\nBefore creating your character, you'll need to decide what kind of World of Warcraft experience you want.\n\nForever replaces the traditional realm-selection process with rulesets that group players around different playstyles.\n\n**Normal:** The best starting choice for most beginners. Focus on exploring, completing quests, joining groups, and learning the game without unwanted player-versus-player combat.\n\n**PvP:** For players who enjoy the additional danger of fighting other players in contested areas.\n\n**Roleplaying:** Ideal if you enjoy character stories, roleplaying, and imagining yourself as part of Azeroth's living world.\n\n**Hardcore:** A special challenge where death has serious consequences. Blizzard plans to introduce this ruleset after launch rather than on day one.\n\nFor your first character, we'd generally recommend Normal. If storytelling and roleplaying are your main interests, the Roleplaying ruleset is worth considering.\n\nRemember that players on different rulesets cannot normally group together, so choose the same ruleset as your friends.\n\n## What Is the Best Class for Beginners in WoW Forever?\n\nOne of your biggest decisions is choosing your class.\n\nYour class determines your combat abilities, the equipment you use, and the role you can play in groups.\n\nWoW Forever has nine classes:\n\n### Warrior\n\nA heavily armed melee fighter who relies on weapons, armor, and physical attacks. Great for players who enjoy direct combat or want to learn tanking.\n\n### Paladin\n\nA holy warrior who combines melee attacks with protective and healing abilities. Suitable for players who like supporting teammates while fighting on the front lines.\n\n### Hunter\n\nA ranged fighter who can use a pet to help in combat. Hunters are traditionally one of the most approachable choices for solo leveling.\n\n### Rogue\n\nA stealth-focused melee fighter who specializes in surprise attacks, positioning, and controlling enemies.\n\n### Priest\n\nA powerful healer who can also use shadow magic. A strong option for players who enjoy healing and supporting groups.\n\n### Mage\n\nA spellcaster who uses Arcane, Fire, and Frost magic. Mages offer powerful ranged attacks, useful utility, and a distinctive fantasy playstyle.\n\n### Warlock\n\nA dark spellcaster who uses curses, damage-over-time spells, and summoned demons. A good choice for players who enjoy magical combat and having a companion.\n\n### Shaman\n\nA spiritual fighter who draws power from the elements. Shamans can use elemental magic, weapons, and healing abilities.\n\n### Druid\n\nA versatile shapeshifter who can take on different animal forms, combining magic, healing, melee combat, and tanking.\n\n### Which class should you actually choose?\n\nFor a brand-new solo player, **Hunter or Warlock are sensible starting choices** because their pets and summoned companions can help manage enemies.\n\nIf you love spellcasting, consider Mage. If you want to support teammates, Priest or Paladin might feel more rewarding. If you want to explore several roles on one character, Druid offers unusual flexibility.\n\nHowever, Forever includes class changes compared with Classic, so old leveling rankings may not perfectly reflect the final release.\n\nMost importantly, don't choose a class only because someone says it is the strongest.\n\nYou're going to spend many hours with this character. Choose the fantasy and playstyle that excites you.\n\n## Alliance or Horde: Which Faction Should You Choose?\n\nWoW Forever has two major playable factions: the Alliance and the Horde.\n\nBoth offer distinctive starting areas, cities, characters, and storylines.\n\nThe Alliance includes races such as Humans, Dwarves, Gnomes, and Night Elves. The Horde includes Orcs, Trolls, Tauren, and the Forsaken.\n\nForever also introduces the Skyborne, with faction-aligned options and an unusual starting story on Zephras Isle.\n\nYour faction determines which players you can group with, which cities you visit, and many of the adventures you'll encounter.\n\nIf your friends are playing, choosing their faction will generally make your experience easier.\n\nOtherwise, choose the faction whose world and identity appeal to you.\n\n## How Leveling Works in WoW Forever\n\nEvery new character begins at level 1, and the main leveling journey continues to level 60.\n\nYou'll earn experience through activities such as defeating enemies, completing quests, and exploring the world.\n\nWhen you gain levels, you'll gradually unlock new abilities and become more capable in combat.\n\nStarting at level 10, you also begin investing in your class's talent trees, allowing you to personalize your character's abilities.\n\nBut leveling in WoW Forever is not supposed to be only a race toward level 60.\n\nMuch of the adventure happens along the way.\n\nYou'll travel between settlements, discover new regions, meet faction leaders, encounter dangerous enemies, and gradually understand why the different peoples of Azeroth are fighting.\n\n### Your first ten levels\n\nDuring your first ten levels, focus on learning the fundamentals:\n\n1. Accept quests in your starting area and read their objectives.\n2. Learn how your main combat abilities work.\n3. Visit your class trainer and check for available abilities.\n4. Keep useful equipment and sell unwanted items to vendors.\n5. Learn how to use your map and find nearby settlements.\n6. Pay attention to the story that connects your quests.\n7. Experiment with your class before worrying about an optimized build.\n\nYou don't need the perfect leveling route on your first character.\n\nIn fact, getting slightly lost can be part of what makes Azeroth memorable.\n\n## Understanding Quests and the Story of Azeroth\n\nQuests are one of the central activities in WoW Forever.\n\nYou might be asked to recover a stolen object, defeat a dangerous creature, investigate a village, or deliver a message across a hostile landscape.\n\nOn the surface, these objectives can seem simple.\n\nBut they're often connected to larger stories.\n\nWhy is a settlement under attack? Who controls the neighboring territory? Why are two factions fighting over an apparently unimportant location?\n\nKnowing the answers makes the world feel much more alive.\n\nFor example, Westfall is more than an area filled with enemies. Its ruined farms, poverty, and criminal activity are connected to the political history of Stormwind and the Defias Brotherhood.\n\nUnderstanding that background completely changes how you experience the zone.\n\n### An easier way to follow the lore while playing\n\nIf you want to understand the story without constantly stopping to read wiki pages, the free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** is designed for that experience.\n\nLore Forever brings narrated stories and contextual lore into the game, helping you understand the places, quests, dungeons, and characters you encounter.\n\nIt can introduce a zone's history when you arrive, provide information about people you meet, and explain the background of your adventures without requiring you to leave WoW.\n\nThe add-on is free forever, works without an account, and supports Windows and Mac. Its most detailed coverage currently focuses on the early leveling experience, with additional place lore for higher-level zones.\n\nIt's particularly useful if you want to experience Azeroth as a world with history rather than simply a collection of objectives.\n\n## How to Earn and Save Gold as a Beginner\n\nGold is the primary in-game currency you'll use for equipment, supplies, training, travel, and other expenses.\n\nDuring your first levels, you probably won't have much gold. That's normal.\n\nThe important thing is learning how to manage what you earn.\n\nStart with a few simple habits:\n\n- Sell unwanted items rather than letting your bags remain full.\n- Avoid buying equipment you don't genuinely need.\n- Compare vendor prices with player-market opportunities when appropriate.\n- Learn a profession that suits your playstyle.\n- Keep enough money for useful abilities, supplies, and future expenses.\n\nYou don't need to become an expert at farming gold immediately.\n\nLearning to manage your resources makes the early game more comfortable, and you'll discover better earning opportunities as you level.\n\n## What Are Professions in WoW Forever?\n\nProfessions let your character gather resources, craft items, and contribute to the game's economy.\n\nFor example, you can become a miner, herbalist, blacksmith, alchemist, tailor, or take up other trades.\n\nForever builds on the original professions system, giving crafting additional importance through updated recipes and the new Camping system.\n\nA profession is more than a method for earning gold. It can also become part of your character's identity.\n\nA wandering herbalist, traveling blacksmith, or experienced alchemist has a natural place in the world of Azeroth.\n\nChoose something that complements how you want to play, rather than worrying about finding the mathematically perfect profession on day one.\n\n## What Is the New Camping System?\n\nCamping is one of Forever's new features.\n\nPlayers can create campsites around outdoor campfires, where adventurers can rest and benefit from useful features and contributions from professions.\n\nDepending on what players bring to a campsite, it may offer utilities such as profession workspaces, repairs, and temporary buffs.\n\nThe system encourages players to interact with the world and one another.\n\nInstead of thinking of every moment between quests as wasted time, a campsite gives you a reason to stop, prepare, and meet other adventurers.\n\nFor players who enjoy immersion, Camping could become one of Forever's most interesting additions.\n\n## What Is the Legacy System?\n\nWoW Forever also introduces the Legacy System, an account-wide progression feature designed to reward the adventures you complete across Azeroth.\n\nLegacy includes challenges, perks, and rewards that recognize activities beyond simply acquiring stronger gear.\n\nIt encourages exploring the world, completing different activities, and experimenting with additional characters.\n\nHowever, you don't need to create multiple characters to benefit. Blizzard says character-related advantages can also be unlocked on a single character.\n\nFor beginners, the most useful thing to remember is simple: your discoveries and achievements can contribute to a larger journey.\n\nYou don't have to rush to understand every Legacy reward during your first few levels.\n\n## How to Make WoW Forever More Immersive\n\nOne of the best ways to enjoy Forever is to treat it as an adventure rather than a checklist.\n\nTake time to explore the villages you visit. Read the occasional quest. Look at the architecture of major cities. Speak to interesting characters. Pay attention to the reasons behind the battles you're fighting.\n\nIf you're curious about the world but don't want to interrupt your gameplay to search for explanations, you can also use **[Willo, the Lore Forever desktop companion](/willo)**.\n\nWillo is a small blue wisp that accompanies you while you play. You can ask her questions aloud and receive spoken answers.\n\nWondering who a character is? Why a city was destroyed? What happened during a particular war? Willo can help you explore those questions as you encounter them.\n\nShe works alongside the free Lore Forever add-on, using information about your in-game context to make conversations more relevant.\n\nWillo is free to start, with optional paid plans for additional voice features and usage. The desktop companion currently requires Windows 10 or 11.\n\nAnd importantly, Willo doesn't play the game for you. You're still the adventurer.\n\n## Common Beginner Mistakes to Avoid\n\nYour first character doesn't need to be perfect. Still, a few habits can make the experience more enjoyable.\n\n**Rushing every quest:** It's easy to focus entirely on experience points and miss the stories connecting the world.\n\n**Ignoring class abilities:** Check what you've unlocked and practice using abilities together instead of relying on the same attack indefinitely.\n\n**Spending all your gold:** Early purchases can feel tempting, but keeping a little currency available gives you more flexibility.\n\n**Choosing a class only for rankings:** A supposedly powerful class isn't necessarily enjoyable for every player.\n\n**Trying to learn everything immediately:** WoW has many interconnected systems. Learn them gradually as you encounter them.\n\n**Playing entirely alone when you need help:** Joining other adventurers can make challenging quests and dungeons more approachable.\n\nAbove all, remember that learning the game is part of the adventure.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### Is WoW Forever beginner-friendly?\n\nWoW Forever is designed to welcome both new and returning players. However, it retains a more deliberate, Classic-inspired approach to exploration, combat, and leveling than modern WoW. Beginners should expect a learning curve, but they don't need extensive Warcraft knowledge to begin.\n\n### Is WoW Forever free to play?\n\nWoW Forever's base game is included with an active World of Warcraft subscription or Game Time. Optional paid upgrades offer additional benefits, and certain content, including the Skyborne starting experience, requires a qualifying edition.\n\n### What is the easiest class for beginners?\n\nHunter and Warlock are traditionally approachable solo choices because they have companions that help in combat. The best choice ultimately depends on the playstyle you enjoy, and Forever's class changes may affect how each class performs.\n\n### Is WoW Forever the same as WoW Classic?\n\nNo. Forever is a separate, permanent WoW experience inspired by the original game. It includes new content, redesigned systems, class changes, and its own continuing storyline.\n\n### Do I need to know Warcraft lore before playing?\n\nNo. You can learn about Azeroth naturally as you complete quests and explore. If you enjoy understanding the stories behind the world, lore resources and in-game narration can make that discovery more rewarding.\n\n### Are add-ons supported in WoW Forever?\n\nYes. WoW Forever supports add-ons through its game client. Compatibility depends on the individual add-on and updates to the game, so choose tools specifically maintained for Forever.\n\n## Begin Your Adventure in Azeroth\n\nWorld of Warcraft: Forever offers an opportunity to discover—or rediscover—what makes Azeroth special.\n\nYou don't need the perfect build, the fastest leveling route, or a complete understanding of Warcraft history before you begin.\n\nChoose a character you love. Explore the world. Meet other players. Follow the stories that interest you.\n\nAnd if you want to understand the world around you without constantly leaving the game, you can bring two companions along:\n\n**[Download Lore Forever — the free WoW Forever lore add-on](/downloads)** to experience narrated stories, discover the history of places and characters, and follow your journey through Azeroth.\n\n**[Meet Willo — your voice companion for WoW Forever](/willo)** to ask questions, hear answers, and explore the world with a little blue wisp by your side. Free to start.\n\nAfter all, the best part of World of Warcraft isn't simply reaching the destination.\n\nIt's everything you discover along the way.\n\n*This guide reflects announced World of Warcraft: Forever features as of October 17, 2026, before the November 4 launch. Mechanics and details may change. For official updates, see [Blizzard's World of Warcraft: Forever page](https://worldofwarcraft.blizzard.com/en-us/forever/). Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made products and are not affiliated with or endorsed by Blizzard Entertainment.*\n",
  "wow-forever-best-class-for-beginners": "---\ntitle: Best Class for Beginners in WoW Forever (2026): All 9 Compared\ndescription: Which class should you play first in WoW Forever? Compare all nine for solo leveling, healing, tanking and immersion, plus which races can play which class.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**Hunter is the safest first class for most solo players in WoW Forever, with Warlock close behind.** Both bring a combat companion, which gives you room to learn how enemies, quests and positioning work. If you want to tank or heal with friends, Paladin and Druid are the most flexible, and Priest is the clearest pick if healing is what excites you.\n\nThis is a beginner-accessibility guide, not a level-60 damage tier list. WoW Forever reworks talents and abilities compared with WoW Classic, and class balance can shift before and after the November 4, 2026 launch. Pick the playstyle you will enjoy first, and use rankings only as a tie-breaker.\n\n## Quick picks by goal\n\n- **First character, mostly solo:** Hunter. Pet support, ranged combat and handy outdoor tools.\n- **Dark fantasy and solo spellcasting:** Warlock. A demon companion, curses and damage over time.\n- **Durable melee with group options:** Paladin. Armor, Holy magic and protection; can tank, heal or deal damage.\n- **Try several roles on one character:** Druid. Shapeshift between caster, healer, melee and tank roles.\n- **Pure caster fantasy:** Mage. Arcane, Fire and Frost, with crowd control and conjured supplies.\n- **Dedicated healing:** Priest. Holy and Discipline healing, with Shadow as a damage option.\n- **Elemental magic and group support:** Shaman. Totems, elemental spells and healing.\n- **Straightforward melee or shield tanking:** Warrior. Weapons, armor and Rage.\n- **Stealth and precision:** Rogue. Ambushes, careful pulls and control.\n\n**How to read difficulty:** here, \"easy\" means forgiving when questing alone, not easy to master at the level cap. Any class can feel complex in group content, and gear, professions and the people you play with often matter more than a single ranking.\n\n## The nine classes, explained for new players\n\n### Hunter: the most approachable solo start\n\nA Hunter fights with a ranged weapon, traps and a pet. The pet takes pressure off you while you practice attacking, repositioning and judging a safe pull, which helps when you are learning the difference between one enemy and three arriving at once.\n\nChoose Hunter if you enjoy wilderness exploration, collecting pets and questing on your own. The classic Hunter talent trees were Beast Mastery, Marksmanship and Survival; Forever reworks talents, so check the in-game trees. **Trade-off:** pets need management, and a pet that runs into extra enemies can ruin a pull.\n\n### Warlock: a forgiving pick for dark-magic fans\n\nWarlocks use curses, damage-over-time spells and summoned demons. A demon helps control enemies while your spells work, so the class suits players who like wearing enemies down instead of relying on one burst attack.\n\nWarlock is a good alternative if you like the independence of a Hunter but want to play a spellcaster. **Trade-off:** you manage health, mana, a demon and several damage effects at once.\n\n### Paladin: durability plus room to change roles\n\nPaladins combine weapons, Holy magic, protection and healing. You can quest as a durable melee fighter and later explore tanking or healing in dungeons. Forever adds the Undead (Forsaken) Paladin, one of its headline new race and class combinations.\n\nChoose Paladin if you like protecting teammates and want more than one group role available. **Trade-off:** tanking or supporting a party still takes awareness of the fight; durability does not replace learning encounters.\n\n### Druid: maximum versatility\n\nDruids shift forms to handle different situations. In Classic, Balance covered ranged magic, Restoration covered healing, and Feral covered melee damage and tanking. Druid suits players who want to experiment instead of locking into one playstyle.\n\nIt is also an immersive class: crossing a forest as a guardian of nature feels different from fighting through it in plate armor. **Trade-off:** several forms and resource systems take time to learn.\n\n### Mage: the classic wizard\n\nMages specialize in Arcane, Fire and Frost. Their strengths are ranged damage, crowd control, conjured supplies and travel spells as they become available. Slowing or freezing an enemy before it reaches you often matters more than casting the highest-damage spell.\n\nChoose Mage if you enjoy positioning and clever ability use. **Trade-off:** poor positioning is punished, and mana management matters in long leveling sessions.\n\n### Priest: a clear healer identity\n\nA Priest can protect and restore allies through Holy or Discipline, or take Shadow as a damage path. Players who like helping a team often find healing rewarding because you see the effect of your choices on the group.\n\nChoose Priest because you want to heal, not because someone says the group needs a healer. **Trade-off:** leveling alone and healing dungeons need different habits, so expect to learn both if you want to do both.\n\n### Shaman: the elements in combat\n\nShamans combine elemental spells, weapon combat, healing and totems. Dwarf Shaman is one of Forever's new race and class combinations. Choose Shaman if you want variety without animal shapeshifting.\n\n**Trade-off:** totems and situational utility add decisions in combat.\n\n### Warrior: weapons, armor and gear upgrades\n\nWarriors fight in melee, build Rage, and can lean toward protection or damage. In a slower, more gear-driven game, a noticeably better weapon is a big part of what makes the class satisfying. Choose Warrior if the straightforward fantasy of a soldier or shield-bearer appeals to you.\n\n**Trade-off:** early solo fights are less forgiving than on a pet class, especially if you pull more enemies than you meant to.\n\n### Rogue: stealth, timing and controlled fights\n\nRogues rely on stealth, Energy, Combo Points, poisons and control. The class rewards planning: pick your target, decide when to engage and keep an escape route.\n\nChoose Rogue if you like tactical play and a less direct style of combat. **Trade-off:** a new Rogue who takes on several enemies without an exit plan has a rough time.\n\n## Best class for solo leveling\n\nFor a first character that levels mostly alone, start with **Hunter or Warlock**. Their companions give you breathing room. A Paladin offers defensive flexibility, a Druid offers several ways to handle danger, and a Mage controls fights well once you understand spacing.\n\n\"Fastest leveling\" is a different question. It depends on final class tuning, your route, travel time, professions, gear and deaths. The public beta started with a level cap of 20 and was raised to 30, so there is no reliable level 1-to-60 speed ranking yet.\n\nIf you are playing for the story, choose the class that makes you curious about the next zone. Minutes saved on a theoretically faster class are not worth much if you dislike playing it for dozens of hours.\n\n## Best class for a group: tank, healer or damage dealer?\n\nTanks keep enemies focused on themselves and protect the party. **Warrior, Paladin and Druid** are the traditional tanking classes. Healers keep the group alive through dangerous moments, and **Priest, Paladin, Druid and Shaman** are the traditional healers. All nine classes can deal damage, in very different ways. Blizzard has not published a role-by-class list for Forever, so confirm specializations in the launch client.\n\nMixed roles make dungeons easier to organize, but nobody has to take a role they dislike. If you want to learn to tank or heal, tell your group you are new and ask for patience.\n\n## Which races can play which class?\n\nWoW Forever widens the race and class combinations of the original game. These lists follow Blizzard's race and class announcement.\n\n**Alliance**\n\n- **Human:** Hunter, Mage, Paladin, Priest, Rogue, Warlock, Warrior\n- **Dwarf:** Hunter, Paladin, Priest, Rogue, Shaman, Warrior\n- **Gnome:** Mage, Priest, Rogue, Warlock, Warrior\n- **Night Elf:** Druid, Hunter, Priest, Rogue, Warrior\n- **Skyborne (High Order):** Druid, Hunter, Mage, Rogue, Warrior\n\n**Horde**\n\n- **Orc:** Hunter, Mage, Rogue, Shaman, Warlock, Warrior\n- **Undead (Forsaken):** Mage, Paladin, Priest, Rogue, Warlock, Warrior\n- **Tauren:** Druid, Hunter, Shaman, Warrior\n- **Troll:** Hunter, Mage, Priest, Rogue, Shaman, Warlock, Warrior\n- **Skyborne (Windshaper):** Druid, Hunter, Rogue, Shaman, Warrior\n\nThe combinations Blizzard names as new are Human Hunter, Orc Mage, Troll Warlock, Undead Paladin, Gnome Priest and Dwarf Shaman, plus the Skyborne race. The [Skyborne guide](/guides/wow-forever-skyborne) covers that race in detail.\n\n## What changed from WoW Classic?\n\nForever keeps the nine-class foundation of the original game, reworks talents and abilities, adds new race and class combinations, and keeps the slower, preparation-focused style of classic combat. It is not a copy of modern WoW's specialization systems. Because talents are reworked, an old Classic leveling ranking may not hold, so verify a specific spell or talent in the release build before building a plan around it.\n\n## Which class feels most immersive?\n\nIf you want to feel like a character living in Azeroth, class identity matters. A Hunter setting out through the forests of Teldrassil has a different fantasy than a Priest looking into the aftermath of Lordaeron. A Druid may feel at home in Ashenvale, a Rogue in the back streets of Stormwind, and a Shaman near elemental trouble. Classes do not replace the main story, but they change the lens you see it through.\n\nTo make leveling more immersive, the free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** puts readable lore and narrated stories into the game, so you can learn about zones, characters and dungeons without tabbing out to a browser. Narration depends on what has been recorded and on the optional voice packs you install. The add-on is free, needs no account, and runs on Windows and Mac. If you would rather ask questions out loud, **[Willo](/willo)** is an optional Windows 10 or 11 desktop companion that works alongside the add-on. It is free to start, has optional paid plans, and answers questions instead of playing the game for you.\n\n## A five-question class picker\n\n1. **Do you want a pet taking some of the pressure?** Try Hunter or Warlock.\n2. **Do you want to heal friends?** Look at Priest, Paladin, Druid or Shaman.\n3. **Do you want to tank?** Look at Warrior, Paladin or Druid.\n4. **Do you want to move in stealth?** Rogue is the obvious first choice.\n5. **Do you love one class fantasy?** Let that settle a close call. Enjoyment lasts longer than any tier list.\n\nIf you are torn, make two characters and play their opening zones. In a game built around the journey, a few hours of experimenting is not wasted.\n\n## Our recommendation\n\nIf you simply want to start playing, **pick Hunter**. If you love magic and summoned creatures, **pick Warlock**. If you want flexible group roles, **pick Paladin or Druid**. If you already have a strong character fantasy in mind, follow it.\n\n## Where to go next\n\nStart with the [WoW Forever beginner's guide](/guides/wow-forever-beginners-guide) for the basics, use the [1–60 leveling guide](/guides/wow-forever-leveling-guide) to plan zones, and read the [Skyborne guide](/guides/wow-forever-skyborne) if the new race interests you.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the easiest class in WoW Forever?\n\nHunter is the easiest class in WoW Forever for most new players, because a pet and ranged combat make early questing forgiving. Warlock is a close second for players who prefer spells and demons. This is a beginner recommendation, not a statement about level-60 damage.\n\n### Is Hunter or Warlock better for solo players in WoW Forever?\n\nHunter is simpler for most beginners because it asks you to manage fewer resources, while Warlock asks you to juggle spells, mana, health and a demon. Both classes can level alone, so the choice comes down to an animal companion or a demon-summoning caster.\n\n### Which classes can heal in WoW Forever?\n\nPriest, Paladin, Druid and Shaman are the traditional healing classes in WoW Forever. Priest is the most dedicated healer, while the other three mix healing with damage or tanking.\n\n### Which classes can tank in WoW Forever?\n\nWarrior, Paladin and Druid are the traditional tanking classes in WoW Forever. Forever reworks talents, and Blizzard has not published a role-by-class list, so confirm specializations in the launch client.\n\n### Which races can be Paladins in WoW Forever?\n\nHumans, Dwarves and Undead (Forsaken) can be Paladins in WoW Forever, according to Blizzard's race and class list. The Undead Paladin is a new combination that the original game did not have.\n\n### What is the best DPS class at level 60 in WoW Forever?\n\nThere is no reliable answer yet. The public beta was capped below level 60, and class tuning can change at launch, so be cautious about any ranking that presents exact damage percentages as settled fact.\n\n### Can I change my class later in WoW Forever?\n\nBlizzard has not announced a way to change the class of an existing character. To try another class, create a new character. Legacy progression and collections are account-wide, so experimenting is not a complete restart.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026, before the November 4 launch. Compare specific spells and talents with the final client. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: Create the Hero You Want to Be](https://worldofwarcraft.blizzard.com/en-us/news/24304075)\n- [Blizzard: Forever beta now live](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24304160/the-world-of-warcraft-forever-beta-now-live)\n- [Warcraft Tavern: Classes and specializations overview](https://www.warcrafttavern.com/forever/guides/classes)\n- [Warcraft Tavern: Race and class combinations](https://www.warcrafttavern.com/forever/guides/race-class-combos)\n",
  "wow-forever-dungeons": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever New Dungeons Guide: All 9 Locations and Levels\ndescription: All nine new WoW Forever dungeons with level ranges, locations and lore: Hall of Thanes, Ruins of Lordaeron, City of Dalaran, Shaper's Terrace and more.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**WoW Forever adds nine new dungeons across the 1–60 journey**, from early adventures in **Hall of Thanes** and **Ruins of Lordaeron** to late challenges such as **Blackmaw Hold** and **Shaper's Terrace**. They expand places that have been part of Azeroth's geography for years.\n\nAn accuracy note: Blizzard confirmed the dungeon names, and its beta announcement confirmed the first two level ranges. The later level bands below come from beta data and community guides, so treat them as provisional and check the in-game recommendation before you travel.\n\n## All nine new dungeons at a glance\n\n- **Hall of Thanes:** levels 13–18, beneath Ironforge. Alliance only.\n- **Ruins of Lordaeron:** levels 15–20, in the ruins of Lordaeron above the Undercity. Both factions.\n- **Excavation Site: Wetlands:** about levels 26–31, above Whelgar's Excavation Site, east of Menethil Harbor.\n- **City of Dalaran:** about levels 28–33, inside Dalaran in the Alterac Mountains. Both factions.\n- **The Drowned City:** about levels 35–40, in Stranglethorn Vale.\n- **Krol'dok Stronghold:** about levels 40–45, in the Riverglades.\n- **Alcaz Prison:** about levels 48–53, in Dustwallow Marsh.\n- **Blackmaw Hold:** about levels 55–60, in Azshara.\n- **Shaper's Terrace:** about levels 58–60, in Un'Goro Crater.\n\nBlizzard has not said which dungeons are open on launch day, so do not assume all nine are available on November 4. Do not treat these numbers as guaranteed minimum entry levels.\n\n## 1. Hall of Thanes: a new story under Ironforge\n\n**Where:** beneath Ironforge. **Levels:** 13–18 (confirmed for beta). **Faction:** Alliance.\n\nThe dwarven capital already feels as if it hides generations of history behind its walls, and Hall of Thanes gives you a reason to descend into its deeper spaces. For a new Alliance character, it is a fitting first group adventure, because the danger lies beneath a city you visit to train and shop.\n\n**Before entering:** learn your group role, bring supplies and look for related quests.\n\n## 2. Ruins of Lordaeron: the aftermath of a kingdom\n\n**Where:** the ruins of Lordaeron, above the Undercity. **Levels:** 15–20 (confirmed for beta). **Factions:** both.\n\nLordaeron is one of Warcraft's central tragedies. Knowing how the kingdom fell, how the Scourge rose and where the Forsaken came from gives this dungeon far more weight than a set of rooms. Blizzard connects it to the Forsaken reclaiming their former homeland, which makes it a story about rebuilding in the shadow of disaster.\n\n**Before entering:** read a short recap of Lordaeron and the Forsaken. See the [lore primer](/guides/wow-forever-lore-explained).\n\n## 3. Excavation Site: Wetlands\n\n**Where:** above Whelgar's Excavation Site in the Wetlands, east of Menethil Harbor. **Levels:** about 26–31.\n\nThe Wetlands have long hinted at archaeology through ruins, digs and dwarven activity. This dungeon takes you deeper than the surface dig sites. Some beta guides list a slightly lower range of 24–29, so rely on the in-game recommendation.\n\n**Before entering:** unlock nearby travel routes and check for related quests.\n\n## 4. City of Dalaran: when magic becomes the danger\n\n**Where:** inside Dalaran, which the Kirin Tor are rebuilding in the Alterac Mountains. **Levels:** about 28–33. **Factions:** both.\n\nDalaran is the city of mages, and Blizzard has teased strange magical trouble inside it. Its bosses include an Arcane Anomaly, an Unstable Sentinel and the Shade of the Archmage. Alliance quests for the dungeon start in Stormwind City, where High Sorcerer Andromath sends adventurers after a Mana Elemental.\n\n**Before entering:** Dalaran has no flight master, so plan your approach. The Alliance Dalaran Skycutter from Zephras Isle reaches its outskirts.\n\n## 5. The Drowned City: pirates, naga and hidden histories\n\n**Where:** the coast of Stranglethorn Vale. **Levels:** about 35–40.\n\nBlizzard connects this dungeon to naga and pirate activity. It suits Forever's mix of geography, danger and competing interests. Wait for quest text and in-game journals before treating any theory about its history as fact.\n\n## 6. Krol'dok Stronghold: a challenge for midlevel groups\n\n**Where:** the Riverglades. **Levels:** about 40–45.\n\nMidway through the journey, players have many questing zones and growing toolkits. Krol'dok tests whether your group can coordinate tougher pulls without relying on raw damage. The Riverglades connect by ship to Tanaris, which helps with travel.\n\n## 7. Alcaz Prison: a darker high-level story\n\n**Where:** Alcaz Island, off Dustwallow Marsh. **Levels:** about 48–53.\n\nA prison dungeon invites questions about who is held there and why. Specific prisoners, villains and rewards should be added only once they are seen in the live game.\n\n**Before entering:** gather your group before the trip and check the recommended level.\n\n## 8. Blackmaw Hold: corruption in the depths\n\n**Where:** Azshara. **Levels:** about 55–60.\n\nBlizzard teases corruption stirring in the depths of Blackmaw Hold. Near the level cap, this is the kind of dangerous challenge that prepares a group for raids.\n\n**Before entering:** bring class consumables and a group that can handle long, hard pulls.\n\n## 9. Shaper's Terrace: a mystery in Un'Goro\n\n**Where:** Un'Goro Crater. **Levels:** about 58–60.\n\nUn'Goro is one of Azeroth's strangest landscapes, a place full of clues that the world is older than most adventurers realize. Blizzard has not published the specific story of Shaper's Terrace, so keep to the confirmed facts until players have seen it.\n\n## How to prepare for your first dungeon\n\nKnow your role. **Tanks** draw enemy attention and position dangerous foes. **Healers** keep the party alive and manage their mana. **Damage dealers** defeat enemies while respecting crowd control and target priorities.\n\nFive habits improve almost every run:\n\n1. Agree on the route.\n2. Give the tank time to establish a pull.\n3. Do not use area damage near controlled enemies.\n4. Stop when the healer needs mana.\n5. Say so if you do not understand a mechanic.\n\nIf you wipe, talk through what went wrong instead of blaming the newest player. Check nearby quest hubs before you travel, because some rewards and story beats come from quests outside the dungeon.\n\n## Plan a dungeon session without wasting an hour\n\nStart with your party's level range, not just the dungeon name. Decide whether the group wants quest completion, a first look at the story or a repeated run for gear. Collect dungeon quests first, then pick a leader.\n\nGroups must share a ruleset and a faction. Cross-ruleset dungeon grouping is not supported, and Alliance and Horde cannot group, so a shared chat channel does not mean your characters can enter together. See the [rulesets guide](/guides/wow-forever-rulesets).\n\nFor the full level-by-level route, use the [leveling guide](/guides/wow-forever-leveling-guide).\n\n## Why dungeon lore is worth learning\n\n\"Clear the Ruins of Lordaeron\" and \"confront the unresolved aftermath of a fallen kingdom\" can describe the same fights, but the second makes them mean something. The free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** gives you readable lore and narrated content where recorded, so you can find that context without leaving the game. The optional **[Willo](/willo)** Windows companion lets you ask questions by voice. Both are fan-made. Neither supplies loot tables or plays encounters for you.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How many new dungeons are in WoW Forever?\n\nBlizzard has announced nine new dungeons for WoW Forever, from the early-level Hall of Thanes to the level 60 Shaper's Terrace. Blizzard has not said which ones are open at launch.\n\n### What is the first new WoW Forever dungeon?\n\nHall of Thanes, beneath Ironforge, is the earliest, with a beta level range of 13–18. Ruins of Lordaeron follows at 15–20.\n\n### Are the WoW Forever dungeon level ranges final?\n\nNo. Only the first two ranges were confirmed in Blizzard's beta announcement. The later ranges come from beta data and community guides, and can change.\n\n### Are the new dungeons replacing the old ones?\n\nNo. Blizzard presents the nine dungeons as additions to the original world, not replacements.\n\n### Can I run WoW Forever dungeons with a friend on another ruleset?\n\nNo. Ordinary cross-ruleset grouping for dungeons is not supported, and groups must also share a faction.\n\n### Can I run these dungeons alone?\n\nThey are designed as group content, so beginners should plan to group.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. Dungeon names are official. Level ranges beyond the first two and entrance details are provisional and need live-game verification. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n- [Blizzard: Forever beta now live](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24304160/the-world-of-warcraft-forever-beta-now-live)\n- [Wowhead: Dungeons overview](https://www.wowhead.com/forever/guide/dungeons-overview-locations-details)\n- [Warcraft Tavern: City of Dalaran dungeon](https://www.warcrafttavern.com/forever/news/city-of-dalaran-dungeon-legacy-points-now-live-in-the-wow-forever-beta/)\n",
  "wow-forever-gold-making": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever Gold Making Guide: 12 Beginner-Friendly Methods\ndescription: Twelve practical ways to earn and save gold in WoW Forever: looting, quests, gathering, the Auction House and professions, with no fake gold-per-hour claims.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**The easiest gold strategy for a new WoW Forever player is to earn money while doing what you already need to do.** Finish quests, loot enemies, sell unwanted vendor items, gather useful materials near your route and spend carefully. Once you understand local prices, the Auction House and professions become extra sources of income.\n\nThis is a pre-launch beginner guide, not a list of proven \"10,000 gold per hour\" farms. Forever's economy will take time to settle after the November 4 launch, and any claim about a best farm needs live prices, real testing and a clear way of measuring it.\n\n## Quick answer: where should beginners start?\n\nLoot everything useful, sell vendor items, complete quests, pick a gathering profession if it fits your route, then test the Auction House with small listings. Keep enough gold for training and necessities before you buy upgrades you will replace in a few levels.\n\n## 1. Loot enemies after every fight\n\nLeaving loot behind is one of the easiest ways to throw money away. Enemies drop coins, equipment, crafting materials and items vendors buy. If your bags fill up, sort them at the next safe stop instead of deleting materials you have not identified.\n\n**Common mistake:** abandoning loot because each drop looks small.\n\n## 2. Sell gray-quality vendor items consistently\n\nA gray item with no use to you is meant to be sold. The important distinction is between a plain vendor item and a tradeable crafting ingredient that other players might pay more for. Read item names and compare prices once you can see the market.\n\n**Common mistake:** keeping worthless items until the bag is full, then deleting something better.\n\n## 3. Complete quest chains instead of farming randomly\n\nQuests give experience, gear, and sometimes money or valuable rewards. Plan objectives in clusters: if a village has three tasks along one road, finish them in a single trip. Fewer return journeys mean more productive play.\n\n**Common mistake:** leaving easy quests behind to chase a high-value farm across the continent.\n\n## 4. Choose a gathering profession early\n\nMining and Herbalism are classic examples. You meet resources as you travel and collect them when your skill allows. A node a few steps from your quest route is worth taking, but a long detour into dangerous enemies can cost more than it pays.\n\n**Common mistake:** following old Classic node routes without checking Forever's updated zones and live demand.\n\n## 5. Collect materials other professions use\n\nCrafters buy materials from other adventurers. You may be carrying valuable components even if you do not craft. Learn which items are in demand, compare prices and reserve bag space for useful stacks.\n\n**Common mistake:** assuming a material stays valuable forever. Demand changes as more players reach different levels.\n\n## 6. Use the Auction House, but start small\n\nThe Auction House connects you with players who need your items. It can beat vendor prices, but a listed item may not sell and prices can move quickly. For your first listings, choose items with visible demand, compare against the vendor price and allow for deposits and fees.\n\n**Common mistake:** buying at a high price because someone says you can resell higher.\n\n## 7. Sell useful equipment you cannot use\n\nYou may loot tradeable gear suited to another class. Before you vendor it, check whether another player might pay more. Do not hold a mediocre item for weeks because an old database calls it rare.\n\n**Common mistake:** treating every green or blue drop as automatically valuable.\n\n## 8. Use professions to make something people need\n\nCrafting is profitable when a finished item is worth more than its materials and costs. The test is simple: **expected sale price minus material cost, fees and the risk it does not sell**. A popular recipe can still lose money if everyone makes it. Check early prices and recipes in game.\n\n**Common mistake:** assuming crafting pays just because an item is powerful.\n\n## 9. Learn what your community needs\n\nAsk guildmates what supplies are hard to find. A small, dependable niche beats chasing whatever is trending on social media. In a guild, one player gathers, another crafts and everyone trades fairly.\n\n**Common mistake:** assuming the same demand exists everywhere at every stage of launch.\n\n## 10. Reduce repair and death costs\n\nGold you do not waste counts too. Avoid fights against enemies far above your level, and take the long safe path when the shortcut is crowded. A few preventable deaths can erase the profit from an efficient route.\n\n**Common mistake:** counting kills while ignoring time, repairs and food costs.\n\n## 11. Buy upgrades selectively\n\nEarly gear is replaced quickly. Ask whether a purchase will really help you finish quests or survive fights. If the difference is tiny, save the gold for training and supplies. Bags are worth buying because they cut vendor trips, but set priorities.\n\n**Common mistake:** treating every small stat gain as urgent.\n\n## 12. Revisit the economy as you level\n\nWhat works at level 15 may not work at 45 or 60. Demand shifts as players learn recipes and prepare for dungeons and the first raids on December 9. Review prices regularly, measure your own results and adjust.\n\n**Common mistake:** copying a months-old guide without checking the present market.\n\n## A simple beginner gold routine\n\nBefore a 60-minute questing session, empty your bags and check supplies. While you quest, loot enemies and gather nodes near your route. In town, sell vendor goods, compare a few tradeable materials at the Auction House and keep a reserve.\n\nTrack three numbers: **gold at the start, gold at the end and major expenses**. If you finish with less gold, work out whether you spent it on training, consumables, gear or market stock. A session with low net gold can still be worthwhile if you earned a level or a profession skill.\n\n## What about \"gold per hour\" farming guides?\n\nA trustworthy gold guide states the game version, test period, character level, profession skills, route, economy conditions, gross revenue, costs and net revenue. Without those, an impressive number is marketing.\n\n## Stay safe: no gold buying, bots or exploits\n\nDo not buy gold from unauthorized sellers, use bots or exploit game systems. Blizzard has said Forever will aggressively act against suspicious gold flow, and it has warned that early on there may be false positives. Blizzard has also described a temporary hold on parts of the economy at launch. Normal questing, gathering and player-to-player trades are the safe place to start.\n\n## Enjoy the world while you earn\n\nIf you optimize every minute around money, the forest becomes a set of nodes. Let a profession enrich exploration instead of replacing it. The free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** gives you readable zone and character background, with narration where recorded, while you travel. **[Willo](/willo)** is an optional Windows voice companion for asking questions about a place. Neither earns gold or automates farming.\n\n## Where to go next\n\nEfficient travel and fewer deaths usually save more gold than an obscure farm. Start with the [leveling guide](/guides/wow-forever-leveling-guide) and the [beginner's guide](/guides/wow-forever-beginners-guide).\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the fastest way to make gold in WoW Forever?\n\nNo fastest method has been verified for the live economy. For new players, questing, looting, gathering and careful selling are the most reliable starting habits.\n\n### Which profession is best for making gold in WoW Forever?\n\nGathering professions suit new players because they fit alongside leveling. The most profitable profession depends on the market, recipes, material costs and what other players buy.\n\n### Should I sell materials to a vendor or the Auction House?\n\nCompare prices first. Some items have little demand and are easy vendor sales, while others sell for much more to crafters. Remember Auction House fees and the chance an item will not sell.\n\n### Can I make gold in WoW Forever without professions?\n\nYes. Quests, enemy loot, vendor sales and tradeable drops all earn gold. Professions add options but are not required.\n\n### Should I buy gold with real money in WoW Forever?\n\nNo. Unauthorized gold sellers break the rules, expose your account to scams and harm the economy.\n\n### Does Lore Forever farm gold for me?\n\nNo. Lore Forever is a free lore add-on, not a gold bot, and Willo is a conversational companion, not a farming service.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. Auction House trends, recipes and profit estimates need checking in the live economy after launch. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n- [Warcraft Tavern: Professions](https://www.warcrafttavern.com/forever/guides/professions)\n- [GamesRadar: Blizzard's approach to suspicious gold trading](https://www.gamesradar.com/games/world-of-warcraft/world-of-warcraft-forever-aims-to-aggressively-shut-down-the-flow-of-suspicious-gold-but-blizzard-warns-there-may-be-false-positives-at-first/)\n",
  "wow-forever-leveling-guide": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever Leveling Guide 1–60: Zones and Tips (2026)\ndescription: Plan your WoW Forever leveling from 1 to 60 with Alliance and Horde zone brackets, the new Riverglades region, dungeon breaks and practical tips.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**The best beginner leveling route in WoW Forever is not one fixed line through Azeroth.** Start in your race's starting zone, follow nearby quests while they match your level, move to a neighboring zone when a quest gets too hard, and add dungeons if you enjoy group play. Forever has fixed-difficulty zones and a level 60 cap, so picking the right area matters.\n\nThis is a pre-launch planning framework, not a tested speedrun. The public beta started with a level cap of 20 and was raised to 30, so many level 31–60 routes and the new high-level areas still need verification in the live game. Blizzard has announced more than 1,000 new quests, so old Classic routes are good starting points but not guaranteed optimal paths.\n\n## WoW Forever leveling zones by bracket\n\n- **Levels 1–12:** Alliance start in Elwynn Forest, Dun Morogh or Teldrassil. Horde start in Durotar, Mulgore or Tirisfal Glades. Skyborne of either faction start on Zephras Isle.\n- **Levels 12–20:** Alliance branch into Westfall, Loch Modan or Darkshore, with Redridge Mountains as you grow. Horde continue into The Barrens, Silverpine Forest or Stonetalon Mountains. The first new dungeons, Hall of Thanes and Ruins of Lordaeron, start here.\n- **Levels 20–30:** Alliance look to Duskwood, Wetlands and Ashenvale. Horde use Ashenvale, Stonetalon Mountains, Hillsbrad Foothills and Thousand Needles. Excavation Site: Wetlands and City of Dalaran open up near the end of this range.\n- **Levels 30–40:** Arathi Highlands, Stranglethorn Vale and Desolace, plus the new Riverglades from the mid-30s. The Drowned City is a dungeon option.\n- **Levels 40–50:** Tanaris, Feralas, the Hinterlands, Badlands and Dustwallow Marsh, with the Riverglades continuing toward the mid-40s. Krol'dok Stronghold is a dungeon option.\n- **Levels 50–60:** Un'Goro Crater, Felwood, Winterspring, the Plaguelands, Burning Steppes and Silithus. Alcaz Prison, Blackmaw Hold and Shaper's Terrace are the late dungeons, and Mount Hyjal is new.\n\nThese brackets adapt original-Azeroth level ranges. They are not a verified fastest route, and not every quest is available at the level listed.\n\n## Before you level: five decisions that save frustration\n\n**Choose the right ruleset.** Normal is the baseline experience and the easiest for most new players. PvP and Roleplaying are different communities. If you are playing with friends, pick the same ruleset and faction, because cross-ruleset dungeon groups are not supported.\n\n**Choose a class you will enjoy.** Hunters and Warlocks offer pet-supported solo play, and Paladins and Druids offer more role flexibility. Time saved by a fast class is wasted if you dislike the next 60 levels. See the [class comparison](/guides/wow-forever-best-class-for-beginners).\n\n**Know your starting area.** Different races start in different places. Do not copy an Elwynn Forest guide onto an Orc in Durotar, and do not assume a Skyborne follows a standard race's first twelve levels.\n\n**Plan supplies and bag space.** A few extra bag slots, food and drink, and affordable repairs cut down on trips back to town. Do not buy expensive upgrades at level 8.\n\n**Accept that the world is dangerous.** Forever has no level scaling. A nearby region can be much harder than your current one, and two enemies of the right level can be a problem for some classes.\n\n## Levels 1–12: learn your character\n\nYour first goal is not speed. It is a reliable way to fight. Learn what your resource bar means, how long you can fight before resting, what your defensive button does and when to retreat.\n\nAlliance Humans commonly start in Elwynn Forest, Dwarves and Gnomes in Dun Morogh, and Night Elves in Teldrassil. For the Horde, Orcs and Trolls start in Durotar, Tauren in Mulgore and Undead (Forsaken) in Tirisfal Glades. New Skyborne characters start on Zephras Isle, Blizzard's level 1–12 starting region, if the account has the Skyborne Heroic Pack or a higher edition.\n\nComplete quests in geographic clusters. When three objectives lie on the same road, do them in one trip, then turn them in together, train abilities and sell items before your bags fill. Do not chase one quest deep into dangerous ground when several suitable quests are nearby. The best route is usually the one with fewer deaths and less backtracking.\n\n## Levels 12–20: pick a second zone and try a dungeon\n\nAlliance players can branch into **Westfall, Loch Modan or Darkshore**, with parts of Redridge Mountains becoming useful as they grow. Horde players often continue into **The Barrens or Silverpine Forest** and use nearby areas as quest availability changes.\n\nThis is a good time to read quest chains as connected stories. Westfall's troubles tie into Stormwind and the Defias Brotherhood, and Silverpine explores the uneasy position of the Forsaken. Quests are more memorable when you know why the villages and factions are involved.\n\nWoW Forever adds two early dungeons. **Hall of Thanes**, beneath Ironforge, is for levels 13–18 and is an Alliance dungeon. **Ruins of Lordaeron**, above the Undercity, is for levels 15–20 and both factions can run it. Blizzard confirmed both level ranges for the beta. Leave extra time to form a group and travel to the entrance, and do not assume repeating a dungeon is always faster than questing.\n\n## Levels 20–30: rotate between quest hubs\n\nIn the twenties, quest chains spread across more of a continent. Alliance players can look at **Duskwood and Wetlands**, and both factions find suitable quests in **Ashenvale and Hillsbrad Foothills**. Horde players can also use **Stonetalon Mountains and Thousand Needles**.\n\nThe key habit is **zone rotation**. When the remaining quests in a zone are above your level, move to another suitable zone, finish its lower quests, then return stronger. Because enemies do not scale to you, forcing a hard quest wastes more time than taking a different road.\n\nTwo new dungeons fall at the end of this range: **Excavation Site: Wetlands**, above Whelgar's Excavation Site east of Menethil Harbor, for about levels 26–31, and **City of Dalaran**, in the Alterac Mountains, for about levels 28–33. Beta guides list slightly different ranges for the Wetlands dungeon, so check the in-game recommendation. Collect flight paths as you find them, because the network pays off after a few zones.\n\n## Levels 30–40: explore beyond the familiar road\n\nTraditional options at this stage include **Arathi Highlands, Stranglethorn Vale and Desolace**. **Riverglades** is one of Forever's biggest additions: Blizzard describes it as a mid-30s to mid-40s region with more than 150 new quests. A new region's quest density can change the best path, even if a nostalgic Classic route looks faster on paper.\n\nThe Riverglades has its own connections, according to Forever's client data. A Steamwheedle ship sails between Steamwheedle Port in Tanaris and Powderfuse Port in the Riverglades, and the zone has new flight points: Farholde Keep for the Alliance and Rog'mar for the Horde.\n\n**The Drowned City**, a dungeon for about levels 35–40 on the coast of Stranglethorn Vale, is a detour for dungeon-focused groups. Blizzard connects it to naga and pirates.\n\n## Levels 40–50: mix familiar zones with new quests\n\nHigher-middle brackets can include **Tanaris, Feralas, the Hinterlands, Badlands and Dustwallow Marsh**, depending on which chains you have finished. A rigid \"one zone per level\" approach becomes inefficient here. Group nearby quests, weigh travel time and avoid crossing a continent for one low-reward turn-in.\n\n**Krol'dok Stronghold** is a new dungeon in the Riverglades for about levels 40–45. **Alcaz Prison**, on Alcaz Island off the northeast of Dustwallow Marsh, is for about levels 48–53. Check each dungeon's recommended level in game before planning a group run.\n\nGather useful profession materials as you travel. Collecting ore or herbs near your quest route can pay for supplies later, but an hour spent hunting one low-value node is poor leveling efficiency.\n\n## Levels 50–60: finish strong without racing blindly\n\nClassic-style options for the last stretch include **Un'Goro Crater, Felwood, Winterspring, Western and Eastern Plaguelands, Burning Steppes and Silithus**. Forever's extra quests may open new routes, so treat these as a menu, not a command.\n\nThe late dungeons are **Blackmaw Hold** in Azshara for about levels 55–60 and **Shaper's Terrace** in Un'Goro Crater for about levels 58–60. **Mount Hyjal** is a new area where players help restore the land after Archimonde's defeat, and it is the home of the new raids. Forever adds flight points there, reached by flying from Everlook in Winterspring to Tainted Foothills. Mount Hyjal is not a confirmed mandatory 50–60 route.\n\nIf you enjoy dungeons, the late levels are a good time to learn group manners: let the tank set the pace, call out crowd control, avoid pulling extra packs and bring the supplies your class needs. Reaching level 60 is a milestone, not the end. Forever's longer-term plans include raids, updates and account-wide Legacy progression.\n\n## Twelve practical leveling tips\n\n1. **Quest in clusters:** finish several objectives before returning to town.\n2. **Avoid repeated deaths:** if an enemy keeps winning, come back later.\n3. **Train new abilities:** visit your class trainer after key level gains.\n4. **Read the map before traveling:** one unnecessary continent crossing can erase small optimizations.\n5. **Unlock flight paths:** the network becomes more valuable over time.\n6. **Keep some gold:** do not spend every coin on a minor upgrade.\n7. **Use the Auction House selectively:** compare likely proceeds after fees and sale uncertainty.\n8. **Carry supplies:** food, water and class reagents prevent avoidable trips.\n9. **Know your escape tools:** surviving a bad pull is a real efficiency gain.\n10. **Group for hard quests:** a short team-up beats several failed solo attempts.\n11. **Do not blindly copy Classic guides:** new quests and class changes matter.\n12. **Enjoy the story:** curiosity makes a long journey easier to sustain.\n\n## Leveling with lore instead of a second monitor\n\nA leveling guide can turn Azeroth into a list of coordinates: you reach a village, accept a quest, kill ten creatures and move on without knowing what the village fears or who sent you. The free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** gives you lore entries and narration, where recorded, for zones, characters, quests and dungeons while you stay in the game. It is free, needs no account, and runs on Windows and Mac. Optional narrator and quest-dialogue packs add more audio.\n\nTo talk a question through while you travel, **[Willo](/willo)** is a separate Windows 10 or 11 voice companion that works alongside the add-on. It is free to start and has optional paid plans. Neither tool chooses quests or plays your character.\n\n## Should you level as fast as possible?\n\nA fast route is satisfying but has trade-offs. Skipping optional quests can mean missing story chains, profession materials and chances to play with friends, and Forever deliberately gives weight to the 1–60 journey. On your first character, we recommend a **balanced route**: finish nearby quests efficiently, take a few dungeon detours and explore a new zone when it sparks your curiosity.\n\nOn a second or third character, you can repeat only the chains you liked and group the fastest quest hubs. Keep separate notes for efficient leveling and lore-rich leveling, because they are not always the same goal. A better measure than raw speed is time spent making useful progress: count travel, repairs, group formation and failed attempts, not just experience gained in a good fight.\n\n## Where to go next\n\nStart with the [beginner's guide](/guides/wow-forever-beginners-guide) and the [class comparison](/guides/wow-forever-best-class-for-beginners). Planning group content? Use the [new dungeons guide](/guides/wow-forever-dungeons). To fund training and travel, read the [beginner gold guide](/guides/wow-forever-gold-making).\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the fastest leveling route in WoW Forever?\n\nNo measured level 1-to-60 route exists yet, because the public beta was capped at level 30. For now, quest in clusters, rotate zones when quests turn red, avoid repeated deaths and cut travel time, then revisit routes once players test the live game.\n\n### What is the level cap in WoW Forever?\n\nThe level cap in WoW Forever is 60. Blizzard plans to add new content inside the existing world instead of raising the cap.\n\n### Which zones should Alliance players level in?\n\nAlliance players start in Elwynn Forest, Dun Morogh or Teldrassil depending on race, then branch into Westfall, Loch Modan or Darkshore. Treat the brackets in this guide as a framework, not a strict itinerary.\n\n### Where should Horde characters level first?\n\nHorde characters start in Durotar, Mulgore or Tirisfal Glades depending on race. The Barrens and Silverpine Forest are common next stops, and later options vary by class and route.\n\n### Is the Riverglades required for leveling?\n\nNo official statement makes the Riverglades mandatory. It is a new region for roughly levels mid-30s to mid-40s with more than 150 new quests, so it is worth visiting for the fresh content.\n\n### Should I do dungeons while leveling in WoW Forever?\n\nDungeons give group play, equipment, quests and variety. Whether they level you faster than questing depends on group quality, travel time and live tuning, so treat them as a change of pace and not a guaranteed shortcut.\n\n### Can an add-on level my character automatically?\n\nNo. Lore Forever is a lore tool, not a bot. It explains stories, but it does not complete quests or choose combat actions.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. Levels above the beta cap and exact quest routes need live-game verification after the November 4 launch. Dungeon levels and travel details come from the beta and Forever's client data and can change. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n- [Blizzard: Forever beta now live](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24304160/the-world-of-warcraft-forever-beta-now-live)\n- [Warcraft Tavern: Forever leveling guides](https://www.warcrafttavern.com/forever/guides/type/leveling/)\n",
  "wow-forever-lore-explained": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever Lore Explained: Story, Timeline and Factions\ndescription: The story of WoW Forever before you play: the setting after Warcraft III, Lordaeron and the Forsaken, who leads the Alliance and Horde, and the Skyborne.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: lore\n---\n**WoW Forever is set in the first year of World of Warcraft, a few years after the Third War and after the Warcraft III: Reforged Forsaken Kingdom campaign, and before Molten Core.** It follows its own timeline: it is not Classic and it does not replay later expansions. The world is recovering from war, old kingdoms have fallen, alliances are fragile and unexplored places are starting to matter again.\n\nYou do not need twenty years of Warcraft knowledge to enjoy it. Four ideas cover the essentials: Azeroth has deep ancient history, the Alliance and Horde are coalitions with their own tensions, the fall of Lordaeron reshaped the human and undead worlds, and Forever extends these stories along new paths.\n\n## WoW Forever's timeline in five steps\n\n- **Ancient Azeroth:** elves, trolls, Titans, elemental forces and old ruins create the world's deep history.\n- **Wars of humans and orcs:** repeated conflicts shape the kingdoms and their alliances.\n- **Warcraft III:** the Scourge, the Burning Legion and the fall of Lordaeron transform the world.\n- **Forsaken Kingdom campaign:** a newer Warcraft III: Reforged campaign explores the Forsaken and the founding of the Undercity.\n- **WoW Forever:** original Azeroth grows along its own timeline, with new regions, quests and mysteries.\n\nThis is an orientation, not a full chronology. Later events from modern WoW should not be treated as facts in Forever's timeline.\n\n## What kind of world is Azeroth?\n\nAzeroth is a world of many peoples, climates, ruins and magical traditions. Its places are more than level brackets: geography reflects earlier migrations, wars and alliances, and old structures keep traces of civilizations that no longer exist as they were.\n\nThe **Eastern Kingdoms** hold human kingdoms, dwarven strongholds and the aftermath of Lordaeron's fall. **Kalimdor** holds wilderness, Night Elf history, the orcish homeland around Durotar and cultures shaped by nature and the elements. Forever returns to these two continents and explores the mysteries and overlooked spaces within them.\n\n## What happened during Warcraft III?\n\nIn broad terms, the Scourge, the Burning Legion, humans, orcs, Night Elves and many other peoples fought devastating wars. Old assumptions about safe kingdoms and lasting alliances collapsed.\n\nThe largest consequence was the **fall of Lordaeron**, once the most powerful human kingdom. On Kalimdor, rival groups had to face a threat greater than their quarrels, and the defeat of **Archimonde** at Mount Hyjal ended the invasion. That matters for Forever because Blizzard has announced new storytelling on Mount Hyjal, where the new raids will open on December 9.\n\nRemember one thing: the world has recent trauma, and its peoples disagree about how to rebuild.\n\n## Why Lordaeron and the Forsaken matter\n\nLordaeron's fall changed the balance between the living and the dead. The **Forsaken** are undead people who broke free of the Scourge and now seek their own path, ruled from the Undercity by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner. They are not simply monsters in a ruined city.\n\nThe Forsaken Kingdom campaign explores the founding of the Undercity and the origins of the first Forsaken Paladin. It is useful background for why Forever adds Undead (Forsaken) Paladins and the **Ruins of Lordaeron** dungeon. Someone who enters those ruins without context sees broken walls and enemies. Someone who knows the story sees a struggle over memory and the chance to reclaim a home.\n\n## The Alliance and the Horde\n\nThe **Alliance** is a coalition of Humans, Dwarves, Gnomes and Night Elves. In Forever's time, Stormwind is ruled by the boy-king Anduin Wrynn, with Highlord Bolvar Fordragon as regent because King Varian Wrynn is missing. King Magni Bronzebeard rules Ironforge, High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind leads the Night Elves, and High Tinker Mekkatorque leads the gnomes.\n\nThe **Horde** brings together Orcs, Trolls, Tauren and the Forsaken. Warchief Thrall leads it from Orgrimmar, Lady Sylvanas rules the Forsaken, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof leads the Tauren, and Vol'jin leads the Darkspear trolls.\n\nNeither side is simply good or evil. Each is a coalition with disagreements and compromises, and individual characters make hard choices. Forever keeps the factions separate, so where you travel and who helps you depends on the side you choose.\n\n## Why the Skyborne are a new chapter\n\nThe **Skyborne** are Forever's headline new race. Their background links ancient Highborne ancestry with elemental wind spirits and a civilization tied to Skywall. Their starting land, **Zephras Isle**, is in crisis: the spirits that guarded them have vanished, structures are failing and communities are drifting apart.\n\nPlayers choose between the Horde **Windshaper** and Alliance **High Order** traditions, and that choice also shapes class options. Windshapers can play Shaman, and High Order Skyborne can play Mage. Read the full [Skyborne guide](/guides/wow-forever-skyborne).\n\n## Four new places and the questions they raise\n\n### Zephras Isle: why did the protective spirits vanish?\n\nThe level 1–12 Skyborne start tells the story of a society whose old agreement with the wind spirits seems to be failing. Its mystery is about ancestry, duty and the future of an isolated people.\n\n### Riverglades: who builds the frontier?\n\nBlizzard describes the Riverglades as a new region of more than 150 quests, roughly for the mid-30s to mid-40s. Rivers, trade routes and old grudges make it a place where local politics can matter as much as world-ending threats.\n\n### Shen'dralas: what remains of forgotten Highborne history?\n\nA new adventure between Mulgore and Desolace ties into the Shen'dralar, the history of Eldre'Thalas (Dire Maul) and the region's centaurs. It asks about a past that has not fully disappeared.\n\n### Mount Hyjal: what happens after a great victory?\n\nInstead of replaying Archimonde's defeat, Forever asks what comes afterwards. How does a wounded land recover, and what dangers remain?\n\n## Does WoW Forever follow the modern WoW story?\n\n**No.** Forever returns to a post-Warcraft III foundation and develops its own continuity, so you should not import a character's later fate from modern WoW. When you read a lore guide, ask whether a claim is shared background, a Warcraft III event, something confirmed for Forever or something from a later timeline. A good article labels each.\n\n## Learn lore while you play\n\nYou do not need days of timelines before your first character. Learn the story of where you stand: in Westfall, why farmers suffer and who the Defias are; in Silverpine, how the Forsaken came to be there; at a strange ruin, who built it.\n\nThat is what the free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** is for. It brings a large lore library into the game and narrated stories where recorded, with optional free voice and quest-dialogue packs. If you prefer to ask questions out loud, **[Willo](/willo)** is an optional Windows companion that works with it and is free to start. It does not turn a theory into canon, so quest text and Blizzard announcements still decide.\n\n## Five lore questions for your first ten levels\n\n1. **Who controls this region?** The answer explains the conflict around you.\n2. **What happened here before I arrived?** Ruined farms and old roads have histories.\n3. **Why is my faction involved?** The Alliance and Horde rarely act without reasons.\n4. **Who are the named characters?** Even small quest givers can connect to larger events.\n5. **What is unresolved?** An open mystery can point to the next zone or dungeon.\n\n## Where to go next\n\nSee [WoW Forever versus WoW Classic](/guides/wow-forever-vs-wow-classic), the [Skyborne guide](/guides/wow-forever-skyborne) and the [new dungeons guide](/guides/wow-forever-dungeons).\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### When does WoW Forever take place?\n\nWoW Forever takes place in the first year of World of Warcraft, a few years after the Third War and after the Forsaken Kingdom campaign, before Molten Core. It follows its own developing storyline.\n\n### Do I need to play Warcraft III before WoW Forever?\n\nNo. Warcraft III gives helpful background, especially for Lordaeron and the Forsaken, but you can start Forever as a first visit to Azeroth.\n\n### Is WoW Forever lore the same as modern WoW?\n\nSome history, peoples and places are shared, but Forever's story is its own. Later events from modern WoW are not Forever canon.\n\n### Who are the Forsaken in WoW Forever?\n\nThe Forsaken are undead people who broke free of the Scourge's control. They are ruled by Lady Sylvanas Windrunner from the Undercity and are working out survival and identity after Lordaeron's fall.\n\n### Where do the Skyborne come from?\n\nBlizzard describes them as descendants of Highborne exiles who made a pact with elemental wind spirits and built a civilization tied to Skywall. Their story begins on Zephras Isle.\n\n### Can I follow WoW Forever lore without reading every quest?\n\nYes. Quest dialogue, zone introductions and tools such as the Lore Forever add-on give context. The add-on offers in-game entries and narration where recorded.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. This article separates the announced Forever setting from later timelines and does not treat unreleased plot details as canon. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n- [Blizzard: Pre-purchase and the Forsaken Kingdom campaign](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24301508/pre-purchase-world-of-warcraft-forever-upgrades-and-begin-your-next-journey-in-azeroth)\n- [Blizzard Watch: How Forever fits Warcraft lore](https://blizzardwatch.com/2026/09/13/world-warcraft-forever-fit-current-lore/)\n",
  "wow-forever-release-date": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever Release Date, Time and Launch Checklist\ndescription: WoW Forever launches November 4, 2026 at 3 PM PST. See launch times by region, what you need to buy, Skyborne access and a ten-step checklist.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**World of Warcraft: Forever launches globally on Wednesday, November 4, 2026, at 3:00 p.m. Pacific Standard Time (PST).** Blizzard announced one global start time. That is 6:00 p.m. Eastern, 11:00 p.m. in London and 12:00 a.m. on November 5 in Paris and Berlin.\n\nBase access is included with an active **World of Warcraft subscription or Game Time**. You do not need a special upgrade to enter Forever, but some benefits, including the **Skyborne race and Zephras Isle experience**, require a paid qualifying edition.\n\n## What time does WoW Forever launch in your region?\n\nThese conversions follow the announced 3:00 p.m. PST launch and assume standard time in each region on November 4.\n\n- **Los Angeles / San Francisco (PST):** 3:00 p.m., Wednesday, November 4.\n- **Denver (MST):** 4:00 p.m., Wednesday, November 4.\n- **Chicago (CST):** 5:00 p.m., Wednesday, November 4.\n- **New York (EST):** 6:00 p.m., Wednesday, November 4.\n- **London (GMT):** 11:00 p.m., Wednesday, November 4.\n- **Paris / Berlin (CET):** 12:00 a.m., Thursday, November 5.\n- **Tokyo (JST):** 8:00 a.m., Thursday, November 5.\n- **Sydney (AEDT):** 10:00 a.m., Thursday, November 5.\n\nDaylight saving rules differ around the world, so check your own city's offset for November 4.\n\nThe announced launch time does not guarantee that everyone connects instantly. Queues, server issues or last-minute maintenance may affect the first hours, and nothing here predicts queue lengths.\n\n## Is WoW Forever free, or do you need to buy it?\n\nFor the **base game**, you need an active WoW subscription or Game Time. Blizzard says Forever access is included with that subscription. That is not the same as free to play.\n\nBlizzard also sells optional upgrades:\n\n- **Skyborne Heroic Pack:** unlocks the Skyborne race and the Zephras Isle starting experience, plus Early Name Reservation and cosmetic extras.\n- **Skyborne Epic Pack:** everything in the Heroic Pack, plus beta access from September 17 and 30 days of Game Time starting November 4.\n- **Warcraft Forever Collection:** a limited-time bundle, available through January 11, 2027, that adds Warcraft III: Reforged and its Forsaken Kingdom campaign to the Epic Pack.\n\nPrices, taxes and regional availability can change, so check the Battle.net shop before you buy. You do not need Skyborne to play a Human, Orc, Night Elf or any other standard race.\n\n## Key dates around launch\n\n- **September 17, 2026:** beta access began for Epic Pack and Collection owners.\n- **October 27 to November 3, 2026:** Early Name Reservation for qualifying upgrade owners.\n- **November 4, 2026:** global launch at 3:00 p.m. PST.\n- **December 9, 2026:** the first announced new raids, subject to roadmap updates.\n\nEarly Name Reservation lets eligible players create and customize up to three characters before launch. Names are first-come, first-served, so read Blizzard's exact terms before relying on a particular name.\n\n## Your ten-step WoW Forever launch checklist\n\n1. **Confirm your Battle.net account.** Make sure you can sign in and reach the email or authenticator tied to your account. Fixing recovery problems on launch day is avoidable.\n2. **Check your subscription or Game Time.** Verify the entitlement ahead of time rather than assuming an old subscription is active.\n3. **Decide whether you want Skyborne.** If you do, you need the Heroic Pack or higher. If you are happy with a standard race, you do not need to buy anything extra.\n4. **Install and update Battle.net.** Follow Blizzard's instructions for the Forever client when downloads open. Never download \"early access clients\" from unofficial sites.\n5. **Pick a ruleset with your friends.** Forever offers Normal, PvP and Roleplaying at launch, with Hardcore planned later. Characters in different rulesets cannot group for dungeons and raids. See the [rulesets guide](/guides/wow-forever-rulesets).\n6. **Choose Alliance or Horde.** Factions stay separate for ordinary grouping, so coordinate your faction as well as your ruleset.\n7. **Decide on a class.** Hunter is a beginner-friendly solo choice, but other classes suit other playstyles. Read the [class comparison](/guides/wow-forever-best-class-for-beginners) instead of chasing speculative rankings.\n8. **Think about your character name.** If you own a qualifying upgrade, use the October 27 to November 3 window. Otherwise, prepare a few alternatives.\n9. **Leave time for game files and add-ons.** Install the files before your planned first session once Blizzard releases them, and use add-ons made for Forever instead of assuming every modern or Classic add-on works unchanged.\n10. **Set one sensible first-session goal.** Finish the starting area, learn your buttons and reach the next town. The world will still be there tomorrow.\n\n## Make your first evening more immersive\n\nThe free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** is built for WoW Forever. It gives you an in-game lore library plus narrated stories where recordings exist, and it is free, needs no account and runs on Windows and Mac.\n\nTo ask questions out loud while you play, **[Willo](/willo)** is a separate Windows desktop companion that works with the add-on. It is free to start, has optional paid plans, and is not required to use the add-on.\n\n## What happens after launch?\n\nForever is a permanent version of original Azeroth with ongoing updates. Blizzard's roadmap includes new raids unlocking from December 9 and more content afterwards. The level 60 journey stays central, so you do not need to rush to endgame on November 4.\n\n## Related guides\n\nRead the [beginner's guide](/guides/wow-forever-beginners-guide) for your first ten levels, the [Skyborne guide](/guides/wow-forever-skyborne) for the edition requirement and the [leveling guide](/guides/wow-forever-leveling-guide) for zone planning.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the exact WoW Forever release date?\n\nWoW Forever launches on Wednesday, November 4, 2026, at 3:00 p.m. PST, which is 23:00 UTC. In many time zones outside the Americas, that is already Thursday, November 5.\n\n### What time does WoW Forever launch in Europe?\n\nThe 3:00 p.m. PST launch is 11:00 p.m. GMT in London and midnight CET on November 5 in Paris and Berlin.\n\n### Is WoW Forever included in a regular WoW subscription?\n\nYes. Blizzard says base access to World of Warcraft: Forever is included with a World of Warcraft subscription or Game Time. Optional race and cosmetic upgrades are sold separately.\n\n### Can I play Skyborne without buying a pack?\n\nNo. Creating a Skyborne in WoW Forever and playing the Zephras Isle starting experience requires the Skyborne Heroic Pack or a higher edition.\n\n### Will my beta character carry over to launch?\n\nBlizzard's beta announcement does not say that beta progress carries over, so do not plan around it. Follow Blizzard's official launch and account notices for the final policy.\n\n### Will Hardcore be playable on November 4?\n\nNo. Blizzard says Hardcore is planned for after launch. Normal, PvP and Roleplaying are the launch rulesets.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. Reconfirm launch, download and access details close to November 4. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: Pre-purchase and global launch](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24301508/pre-purchase-world-of-warcraft-forever-upgrades-and-begin-your-next-journey-in-azeroth)\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n",
  "wow-forever-rulesets": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever Rulesets Explained: Normal, PvP, RP and Hardcore\ndescription: Which WoW Forever ruleset should you pick? Compare Normal, PvP, Roleplaying and Hardcore, including grouping with friends, factions and switching.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**For most first-time WoW Forever players, choose the Normal ruleset.** It lets you learn quests, dungeons, professions and the world without a setting built around frequent open-world fights between players. Choose **PvP** for danger from the opposing faction, **Roleplaying** if you enjoy acting as your character, and **Hardcore** only once it arrives after launch and you accept lasting consequences for death.\n\nThe key change from older realm selection is that Forever starts by asking how you want to play, then places you in that ruleset's community. Blizzard says the rulesets are distinct communities, not traditional individual realms.\n\n## WoW Forever rulesets compared\n\n- **Normal:** questing, dungeons, exploration and optional PvP. Best for most beginners and cooperative groups. Available at the November 4 launch.\n- **PvP:** open-world faction conflict in contested territory. Best for players who enjoy danger and world PvP. Available at launch.\n- **Roleplaying (RP):** an immersive, character-driven social community. Best for roleplayers and story-focused players. Available at launch.\n- **Hardcore:** a high-stakes journey where death has lasting consequences. Best for experienced players. Not available at launch; planned for after.\n\nThe real question is not which ruleset gives the best loot, but which community and rules make the game fun for you.\n\n## Normal: the best default for new players\n\nNormal is the baseline ruleset. You focus on leveling, exploring, professions, dungeons and cooperative play, and PvP is something you choose to do. It suits mixed-skill groups of friends, players who want to read stories without interruption, and anyone new to the slower pace of classic-style WoW.\n\n## PvP: the world itself becomes more dangerous\n\nOn the PvP ruleset, traveling through contested territory can expose you to hostile players, which changes how you plan routes and whether you travel alone. That can create memorable moments, such as defending an ally or escaping an ambush, and it can frustrate you if you want a peaceful quest chain.\n\n**Important restriction:** Blizzard says that on the PvP ruleset you can create characters for only one faction. If you begin as Alliance there, you cannot create Horde characters on the same PvP ruleset, and the reverse applies too. Agree on a faction with your friends before you commit.\n\n## Roleplaying: experience Azeroth as your character\n\nRoleplaying is about inhabiting a character: a wandering merchant, an anxious apprentice mage, a Forsaken learning about a new life. RP communities value names, stories and social events. You do not have to speak in character constantly or know every piece of lore, but you should respect players who chose the ruleset for that experience.\n\nFor lore-focused players, RP is a natural fit, because a quest through Lordaeron means more when you imagine what your character knows about that kingdom.\n\n## Hardcore: a challenge arriving later\n\nHardcore gives death serious and lasting consequences. Blizzard has said it will arrive after the November 4 launch, not with the first rulesets.\n\nHardcore stays separate from the others. Blizzard also says a Hardcore character that dies can move to another ruleset, including PvP, instead of resuming Hardcore progress. If this is your first time in WoW, learn on Normal first.\n\n## Can friends on different rulesets play together?\n\n**Not for ordinary quest-group, dungeon or raid activity.** Blizzard says each ruleset is its own space, and cross-ruleset grouping is not supported for those activities.\n\nBattlegrounds are a partial exception. Blizzard says battleground matches may include players from other rulesets, with Hardcore kept separate.\n\nFaction is a second boundary. Horde and Alliance do not group in ordinary Forever content, even on the same ruleset. The safe planning rule is: **same faction and same ruleset if you want to quest and run dungeons together.**\n\n## Can you switch rulesets later?\n\nNot on an existing character. Blizzard says you ordinarily need a new character in the ruleset you want to join. Hardcore has a special death-related transfer path, but it is not a free transfer system. Collections and Legacy points can be shared across Normal, PvP and Roleplaying under Blizzard's announced rules, while Hardcore has stricter one-way conditions.\n\nSettle the choice with your friends and guild before you create a long-term character.\n\n## Which ruleset is most immersive?\n\nRoleplaying is the clearest choice for invented histories and in-character conversation. Normal can be just as immersive if you prefer quiet exploration and story quests. PvP creates intense, unpredictable journeys. No ruleset is best for everyone.\n\nTo follow the story while you play, the free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** offers a lore library and narration where recorded, and it works in game. **[Willo](/willo)** is a separate Windows app for asking questions by voice and is free to start. Neither changes your ruleset or plays your character.\n\n## Five things to decide before creating your character\n\n1. **Who are you playing with?** Agree on the same ruleset.\n2. **Which faction will they pick?** Alliance and Horde cannot form ordinary mixed groups.\n3. **Do you enjoy open-world PvP?** If not, pick Normal or RP.\n4. **Do you want in-character community?** RP may be your home.\n5. **Would permanent stakes make the game more or less fun?** If unsure, do not make Hardcore your first experience.\n\nChoosing a ruleset you dislike gives you no advantage. It is a social and gameplay preference, not a test of whether you are a real Warcraft player.\n\n## Where to go next\n\nAfter you choose a ruleset, read the [beginner's guide](/guides/wow-forever-beginners-guide) and the [class comparison](/guides/wow-forever-best-class-for-beginners). The [launch checklist](/guides/wow-forever-release-date) lists the dates and account steps for playing with friends.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is the difference between Normal and PvP in WoW Forever?\n\nNormal focuses on exploration and cooperation, with optional PvP. The PvP ruleset makes contested territory more dangerous because opposing players are part of the challenge.\n\n### Which WoW Forever ruleset is best for beginners?\n\nNormal is the best ruleset for beginners. It lets you learn combat, quests and travel without the added pressure of world PvP.\n\n### Can Normal and Roleplaying characters do dungeons together in WoW Forever?\n\nNo. Ordinary cross-ruleset grouping is not supported, so friends who want to quest and run dungeons together should choose the same ruleset and faction.\n\n### Can I play both Horde and Alliance on the PvP ruleset?\n\nNo. Blizzard says that on the PvP ruleset you can create characters for only one faction.\n\n### Is Hardcore available on WoW Forever launch day?\n\nNo. Blizzard has announced Hardcore as a post-launch addition, so the launch rulesets are Normal, PvP and Roleplaying.\n\n### Can I transfer a character to another WoW Forever ruleset?\n\nOrdinarily no. You create a new character in the ruleset you want. Blizzard has described a separate transfer path for Hardcore characters that die.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. Recheck Hardcore availability and transfer rules after launch. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: Choose your ruleset](https://worldofwarcraft.blizzard.com/en-us/news/24302070)\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n",
  "wow-forever-skyborne": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever Skyborne Guide: Classes, Unlock and Zephras Isle\ndescription: How to unlock the Skyborne in WoW Forever, choose Horde Windshaper or Alliance High Order, compare classes and racials, and start on Zephras Isle.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**The Skyborne are a new elf-descended playable race in WoW Forever with two faction paths: Horde-aligned Windshapers and Alliance-aligned High Order Skyborne.** Both begin on **Zephras Isle**, a new level 1–12 region. Warrior, Hunter, Rogue and Druid are open to both paths. The Horde path can also play Shaman, and the Alliance path can also play Mage.\n\nOne detail matters before you plan a Skyborne character: **the race and its starting experience are not included with a base subscription alone.** You need the **Skyborne Heroic Pack or a higher edition**.\n\n## Skyborne quick facts\n\n- **What they are:** a new playable race in World of Warcraft: Forever.\n- **Starting zone and levels:** Zephras Isle, levels 1–12.\n- **Horde tradition:** Windshaper.\n- **Alliance tradition:** High Order.\n- **Classes for both:** Warrior, Hunter, Rogue and Druid.\n- **Horde-only class:** Shaman.\n- **Alliance-only class:** Mage.\n- **Access:** Skyborne Heroic Pack or a higher edition.\n- **Game launch:** November 4, 2026.\n\nThe race adds new customization options, voices, animations and Druid forms. Racial abilities, listed below, can still be tuned before and after launch.\n\n## How to unlock the Skyborne in WoW Forever\n\nYou cannot unlock the Skyborne by leveling another race or finishing an achievement, based on Blizzard's announcement. Blizzard ties **the race and the Zephras Isle starting experience to a paid upgrade**.\n\nTo prepare, make sure you have a Battle.net account and an active World of Warcraft subscription or Game Time for the base game. Then, if you want Skyborne, buy the **Skyborne Heroic Pack** or a higher bundle from Blizzard. Once the entitlement is on your account, you can create the race when character creation allows it.\n\n- **Skyborne Heroic Pack:** the Skyborne race and Zephras Isle starting experience, Early Name Reservation, and extras including a ground mount and cosmetics.\n- **Skyborne Epic Pack:** everything in the Heroic Pack, plus beta access from September 17 and 30 days of Game Time starting November 4.\n- **Warcraft Forever Collection:** a limited-time bundle, available through January 11, 2027, that adds Warcraft III: Reforged and its Forsaken Kingdom campaign to the Epic Pack.\n\nCheck prices and availability in the Battle.net shop, because offers can vary by region. **The free Lore Forever add-on does not unlock Skyborne or any other game content.** It is an independent fan-made lore tool.\n\n## Windshaper or High Order: which Skyborne should you choose?\n\nBoth traditions share an ancient ancestry but disagree about their history and their future. You pick the tradition, and with it your faction, at character creation.\n\n### Horde: Windshaper Skyborne\n\nWindshapers descend from the first shen'dorei high elves to receive the wind spirits' gift of elemental skysight, and their elemental link shows in **Shaman** access. Their classes are **Warrior, Hunter, Rogue, Druid and Shaman**. Choose this path if you want to adventure with Horde friends or specifically want a Skyborne Shaman.\n\n### Alliance: High Order Skyborne\n\nHigh Order Skyborne descend from the ancient Highborne scholars of Eldre'Thalas, which shows in **Mage** access. Their classes are **Warrior, Hunter, Rogue, Druid and Mage**. Choose this path if your friends play Alliance or you want an arcane Skyborne Mage.\n\n**Practical decision:** faction matters more than small racial differences when you coordinate with friends. Horde and Alliance characters cannot join the same ordinary dungeon group in Forever, and ruleset choices add a second separation.\n\n## Skyborne racial abilities\n\nBlizzard's Skyborne announcement lists these racials. Treat the numbers as launch-version values that may be tuned.\n\n- **Windshaper (Horde):** Walk on Air (glide downward for 10 seconds), Skysight (an Elemental Blessing worth +10% run speed), Wind Blessed (+1% melee, ranged and spell Haste) and Elemental Insight (+5% damage to Elementals).\n- **High Order (Alliance):** Walk on Air, Read Ley Line (activate a ley line for 100% increased Health and Mana regeneration), Wind Blessed and Elemental Insight.\n\n## Skyborne classes compared\n\n- **Warrior:** both paths. Weapon combat and tanking.\n- **Hunter:** both paths. Solo questing and pet play.\n- **Rogue:** both paths. Stealth and deliberate combat.\n- **Druid:** both paths. Shapeshifting, healing and flexibility.\n- **Shaman:** Horde Windshaper only. Elements, totems and healing.\n- **Mage:** Alliance High Order only. Arcane, Fire and Frost spells.\n\nSkyborne cannot be Paladins, Priests or Warlocks, so do not assume all nine classes are open to every race.\n\nIf this is your first WoW character, a Skyborne Hunter is a reasonable choice because the pet makes solo fights more forgiving. A Druid suits you if you want several roles, and Shaman and Mage deliver the most faction-specific character fantasies.\n\n## Where do the Skyborne start? Exploring Zephras Isle\n\nEvery Skyborne begins on **Zephras Isle**, a region for roughly levels 1 through 12 whose architecture draws on **Skywall**, the elemental plane of air. Blizzard frames it as a starting adventure about wind, ancestry, separation and a society under pressure.\n\nAccording to Blizzard's race introduction, Skyborne history reaches back thousands of years to **Highborne exiles who made a pact with elemental wind spirits** and built a civilization in Skywall. Now the spirits that protected the homeland have vanished, ancient structures are failing, communities are isolated and the Skyborne are divided.\n\nThat makes the race more than a cosmetic option. The starting area sets up a conflict you play through, and your choice of Windshaper or High Order reflects a different response to the crisis.\n\n## What should you do during levels 1–12?\n\nFollow the opening quest chain, learn your class's first abilities, pick up gear as you earn it and do not skip the dialogue that explains why the island is in trouble. Check your trainer when new abilities unlock.\n\nThe main learning goals are your class resource, your main attack, a defensive or escape tool, and how to judge whether an enemy is too dangerous. Druids should practice switching forms, Hunters should learn pet management as it unlocks, and Shamans and Mages should learn to manage mana between fights.\n\n**Leaving Zephras Isle:** according to Forever's client data, two airships sail from Valanaar on the island. The Horde airship goes to Skywatcher Plateau in Mulgore, near Thunder Bluff, and the Alliance's Dalaran Skycutter goes to the outskirts of Dalaran in the Alterac Mountains. The island itself has no flight master.\n\n## Why the Skyborne matter to Forever's story\n\nForever is not replaying the whole timeline of modern WoW. It returns to the foundational conflicts and geography of original Azeroth and extends its own story. The Skyborne give a different view of the Highborne legacy, arcane ambition and relations with the elements. The Windshaper and High Order split works as storytelling because two characters from one people can hold very different views of power and duty, which makes the faction choice part of the narrative. When you finish Zephras Isle, you carry a history the rest of Azeroth may not know.\n\n## Make the starting story feel like an adventure\n\nIf you like the background of a zone but do not want to keep opening a wiki, try the free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)**. It includes a library of lore entries and narration where recorded, shown in an in-game interface. Voice and quest-dialogue coverage varies by area and optional pack, so check the downloads page for current coverage, including newly introduced areas.\n\nOn Windows, **[Willo](/willo)** is a separate desktop companion that works alongside the add-on, so you can ask questions about the world out loud. It is free to start, has optional paid plans, and does not unlock the Skyborne race or play the game for you.\n\n## Where to go next\n\nIf you are choosing between a Skyborne Hunter, Mage, Shaman or Druid, compare the [nine beginner classes](/guides/wow-forever-best-class-for-beginners). For the Highborne, the wind spirits and the wider timeline, read the [WoW Forever lore primer](/guides/wow-forever-lore-explained).\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Are Skyborne free with a WoW subscription?\n\nNo. The base WoW Forever game is included with a subscription or Game Time, but creating a Skyborne and playing the Zephras Isle starting experience requires the Skyborne Heroic Pack or a higher edition.\n\n### Can Skyborne be both Alliance and Horde?\n\nYes. The Skyborne have a Horde path (Windshaper) and an Alliance path (High Order). Each character chooses one at creation.\n\n### Can a Skyborne be a Paladin or Priest?\n\nNo. Both Skyborne paths can be Warrior, Hunter, Rogue or Druid. Windshapers can also be Shaman and High Order Skyborne can also be Mage. Paladin, Priest and Warlock are not available to the Skyborne.\n\n### What level is Zephras Isle?\n\nZephras Isle is the Skyborne starting region for levels 1 through 12.\n\n### Is Skyborne good for a first character?\n\nYes, if you like the race's story and have the required edition. A Skyborne Hunter is approachable for solo beginners, and a Druid gives you several role options.\n\n### Can I earn the Skyborne through a quest later?\n\nBlizzard has not announced any quest-based unlock. Check Blizzard's account instructions for current availability before you buy or plan.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. Racial numbers and quest routes can change; check them against the launch client. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: Meet the new Skyborne](https://news.blizzard.com/en-gb/article/24302071/wow-forever-meet-the-new-skyborne)\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n- [Blizzard: Pre-purchase and edition requirements](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24301508/pre-purchase-world-of-warcraft-forever-upgrades-and-begin-your-next-journey-in-azeroth)\n",
  "wow-forever-vs-wow-classic": "---\ntitle: WoW Forever vs WoW Classic: 12 Key Differences (2026)\ndescription: WoW Forever vs Classic: how leveling, zones, Skyborne, classes, rulesets, dungeons, Legacy and Camping differ, and which version you should play.\ndate: 2026-10-17\nkind: tutorial\n---\n**WoW Classic lets you revisit older versions of World of Warcraft, while WoW Forever builds new, continuing adventures on the foundation of original Azeroth.** Forever keeps the level cap at 60 and the focus on travel, preparation and social play, and adds zones, quests, dungeons, class updates and account-wide progression.\n\nBlizzard describes Forever as a permanent experience rather than a limited season or another Classic version. This comparison is mainly with **WoW Classic Era**, the persistent original-style game. Other Classic progression and seasonal versions are separate products, so their systems can differ.\n\n## WoW Forever vs Classic at a glance\n\n- **Overall idea:** Classic Era preserves the original-style game. Forever expands original Azeroth permanently.\n- **Level cap:** 60 in both.\n- **World:** Classic Era has the established regions. Forever keeps them and adds new and reworked areas.\n- **Questing:** Forever adds more than 1,000 new quests across the 1–60 journey.\n- **Races:** Forever adds the Skyborne, which requires a paid upgrade.\n- **Classes:** Nine classes in both, with reworked abilities and talents in Forever.\n- **Race and class combinations:** Forever adds several, including the Undead Paladin.\n- **Dungeons:** Forever adds nine new dungeons to the original ones.\n- **Progression:** Forever adds the account-wide Legacy system.\n- **Outdoor social play:** Forever adds Camping, which links campfires to professions.\n- **Starting choice:** Forever uses rulesets (Normal, PvP and Roleplaying, with Hardcore later) instead of picking a realm.\n- **Look and input:** Forever adds visual presets, HD and SD character models, opt-in transmog and official gamepad support.\n\nThis describes announced features before launch. Beta details can still change.\n\n## 1. Forever is designed to keep growing\n\nClassic Era appeals because its game stays recognizable. Forever promises something different: new adventures built on that foundation over time, without treating the first release as a temporary experiment.\n\nBlizzard's announced roadmap includes two new raids on Mount Hyjal that unlock on December 9 (the 10-player Barrow Deeps and the 20-player Hyjal Summit), plus more raids, dungeons, PvP updates, world content, quests and a revamped iconic raid after launch. Blizzard has also announced a new 15 vs 15 battleground, the Darkspear Islands. If you are deciding whether to invest hundreds of hours in a new character, this is the main reason to pick Forever.\n\n## 2. The world starts familiar, but new routes open\n\nThe Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor remain central. Forever reaches into previously unexplored or underdeveloped places, including **Zephras Isle, the Riverglades, Shen'dralas and Mount Hyjal**. The goal is to enrich the space between familiar places, not replace them.\n\nFor a returning player that means you can recognize the road without knowing what lies beyond the next turn. For a new player, the additions should feel like one world rather than a string of disconnected expansions.\n\n## 3. More than 1,000 new quests expand leveling\n\nBlizzard has announced more than one thousand new quests across the 1–60 journey. That does not make every old route useless or mean everyone follows one optimal path. It means more places to explore and more story threads on the way to level 60. The Riverglades alone brings more than 150 new quests for roughly the mid-30s to mid-40s.\n\nThe fastest route cannot be proven from a pre-launch beta capped at level 30, so expect player routes to change once live leveling data exists. The [leveling guide](/guides/wow-forever-leveling-guide) has a zone-by-zone framework.\n\n## 4. A new playable race: Skyborne\n\nSkyborne start on **Zephras Isle**, a new level 1–12 starting region. The race has two faction traditions: Horde Windshapers and Alliance High Order Skyborne. Both can be Warrior, Hunter, Rogue or Druid. The Horde side adds Shaman, and the Alliance side adds Mage.\n\n**Important:** the Skyborne race and the Zephras Isle starting experience require the Skyborne Heroic Pack or a higher edition. An ordinary subscription or Game Time gives base access to Forever, not this race. See the [Skyborne guide](/guides/wow-forever-skyborne).\n\n## 5. Nine classes, with reworked gameplay\n\nForever keeps the nine-class lineup: Warrior, Paladin, Hunter, Rogue, Priest, Mage, Warlock, Shaman and Druid. Talents and abilities are updated while the classic class identities stay. That makes class guides worth rereading even if you played Classic for years. Our [class comparison](/guides/wow-forever-best-class-for-beginners) covers the beginner picks.\n\n## 6. More race and class combinations\n\nClassic's race and class restrictions were part of its identity. Forever adds **Human Hunter, Orc Mage, Troll Warlock, Undead Paladin, Gnome Priest and Dwarf Shaman**, plus the Skyborne options.\n\nThis is not only mechanical. An Undead (Forsaken) Paladin suggests character stories and tensions that a traditional Alliance Paladin does not. If you enjoy roleplay, the new combinations are a strong reason to try Forever.\n\n## 7. Nine new dungeons\n\nBlizzard has announced nine new dungeons: Hall of Thanes, Ruins of Lordaeron, Excavation Site: Wetlands, City of Dalaran, The Drowned City, Krol'dok Stronghold, Alcaz Prison, Blackmaw Hold and Shaper's Terrace. Blizzard has confirmed level ranges only for the first two, Hall of Thanes at levels 13–18 and Ruins of Lordaeron at levels 15–20.\n\nThe full list, with locations and likely level bands, is in the [dungeons guide](/guides/wow-forever-dungeons).\n\n## 8. Legacy rewards more than one character's path\n\nLegacy is a new account-wide progression system for broader adventure and character activity. Blizzard highlights players who love creating alternate characters, and also says one-character players can unlock character-related advantages.\n\nFor newcomers, this can make trying another class feel less like starting over. For veterans, it adds long-term goals beyond one raid or one piece of gear.\n\n## 9. Camping adds a social layer to the outdoors\n\nCamping lets adventurers craft campfires in the outdoor world, where other players can stop by, contribute through professions and share campsite buffs. It may sound small next to a raid, but it matches Forever's philosophy of giving players reasons to pause, prepare and meet each other outside cities and dungeons. Exact buffs and the best uses are worth revisiting after launch.\n\n## 10. Rulesets replace the realm decision\n\nInstead of picking a realm, you pick a **ruleset**: Normal, PvP or Roleplaying, with Hardcore arriving after launch. Blizzard describes each as a broad player ecosystem rather than a traditional realm.\n\nCross-ruleset grouping for dungeons and raids is not supported, and Alliance and Horde characters cannot group together. That makes the ruleset choice important if you want to play with specific friends. The [rulesets guide](/guides/wow-forever-rulesets) explains each one.\n\n## 11. Travel and danger stay important\n\nForever keeps a world with **no flying mounts and no level scaling**. Geography, danger and the journey between places still matter, and you cannot count on zones adjusting to your level as you walk in. Preparation, class tools and cooperation stay valuable, and a long journey can still be part of the adventure instead of a commute.\n\n## 12. More visual choice without dropping the classic look\n\nForever includes modern and Classic visual presets, HD and SD character models, restored Classic animations for HD models, opt-in transmog, rendering improvements such as rivers in Elwynn Forest, and official gamepad support. Players choose how the world looks instead of everyone getting one modern presentation, and the game is not visually identical to Classic.\n\n## Which version should you play?\n\n**Choose Classic Era** if you want the preserved original-style experience, familiar progression and an established community around that specific game. **Choose Forever** if you want the older world's scale and danger plus new quests, stories, places and systems that keep growing.\n\nIf you are coming from modern WoW and want a slower journey, Forever is a natural entry point. If you are rejoining friends, play the version they actually play. The best version on paper is not much fun if your group is somewhere else.\n\n## Follow the new stories without losing immersion\n\nOne of Forever's strengths is unfamiliar stories in familiar landscapes. The free **[Lore Forever add-on](/downloads)** lets you read about zones, characters and dungeons, with narration where recorded, without tabbing out of the game. It is free, needs no account, and runs on Windows and Mac. If you prefer asking questions while you play, **[Willo](/willo)** is an optional Windows 10 or 11 desktop companion that works alongside the add-on. It offers voice conversations, is free to start, has optional paid plans, and does not play your character.\n\n## Where to go next\n\nStart with the [beginner's guide](/guides/wow-forever-beginners-guide), compare the [Skyborne race options](/guides/wow-forever-skyborne), and read the [lore primer](/guides/wow-forever-lore-explained) before your first quest. Choosing a [ruleset](/guides/wow-forever-rulesets) together with friends is the next practical step.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is WoW Forever the same as Classic Plus?\n\nWoW Forever resembles the long-discussed \"Classic+\" idea of expanding original Azeroth, but Blizzard presents it as its own permanent experience, not a Classic patch or season.\n\n### Is WoW Forever replacing WoW Classic?\n\nBlizzard has not announced that WoW Forever replaces WoW Classic. They are separate products with different goals, so choose the version you want to play.\n\n### Is WoW Forever free if I already subscribe?\n\nBase access to WoW Forever is included with an active World of Warcraft subscription or Game Time. Optional upgrades, including the Skyborne Heroic Pack needed to play the Skyborne race, are sold separately.\n\n### Does WoW Forever have the same level cap as Classic?\n\nYes. WoW Forever keeps the 1–60 leveling journey, with new content added inside that world instead of a rising level cap.\n\n### Will I have to relearn my class in WoW Forever?\n\nPartly. Abilities and talents have changed, so review your class before you play. The identity of each of the nine classes stays recognizable.\n\n### Does WoW Forever have flying mounts?\n\nNo. Blizzard has said Forever's design has no flying mounts and no level scaling.\n\n## Sources\n\n*Reviewed October 17, 2026. This is a pre-launch comparison; features can change at or after the November 4 launch. Lore Forever and Willo are fan-made and are not affiliated with Blizzard Entertainment.*\n\n- [Blizzard: What's Next panel recap](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24303862/world-of-warcraft-forever-whats-next-panel-recap)\n- [Blizzard: Choose your ruleset](https://worldofwarcraft.blizzard.com/en-us/news/24302070)\n- [Blizzard: Pre-purchase and subscription access](https://news.blizzard.com/en-us/article/24301508/pre-purchase-world-of-warcraft-forever-upgrades-and-begin-your-next-journey-in-azeroth)\n"
};
